WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.140
Ray optics helped us locate images.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.120
Now we need wave optics to explain bright and dark bands.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.720
A wavefront is a surface joining points with the same phase.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:16.320
A point source in a uniform medium produces spherical wavefronts; far from a

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.140
source, a small patch can be treated as a plane wavefront.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.880
In an isotropic medium, rays point perpendicular to the wavefront in the

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:24.640
direction of travel.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.000
Huygens's principle treats every point on an existing front as a source of

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.400
secondary wavelets.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.760
After a short time, each wavelet has radius speed times that time.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.520
The advancing envelope gives the next wavefront.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.060
This is a construction for propagation, not a claim that we have installed a

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.560
separate physical lamp at every point.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.780
For a plane wave in a uniform medium, all those radii are equal, and the new

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.320
envelope is another parallel plane.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.916
In a medium of lower phase speed, the wavelets grow more slowly during the

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:55.140
same

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.600
time interval.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.160
That difference will let us derive refraction rather than merely quote Snell's

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:00.280
law.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.080
It also explains why apertures can spread light into directions that a single

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.320
straight ray sketch misses.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.060
The full quantitative treatment needs superposition and phase information;

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.720
Huygens's simple picture is our conceptual starting point, not a complete

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.400
intensity calculation by itself.

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:20.556
Let a tilted plane wavefront reach a flat mirror.

00:01:21.576 --> 00:01:25.256
One end reaches the mirror first and begins contributing reflected wavelets

00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:27.076
while the other end is still approaching.

00:01:28.036 --> 00:01:31.936
During the same time interval, both incident and reflected light travel at the

00:01:31.936 --> 00:01:33.456
same speed in the same medium.

00:01:34.936 --> 00:01:38.976
The two construction triangles have equal traveled distances, and geometry

00:01:38.976 --> 00:01:41.236
gives equal incidence and reflection angles.

00:01:42.056 --> 00:01:44.436
That is the ray law recovered from wavefronts.

00:01:45.416 --> 00:01:49.036
For refraction, repeat the construction at a boundary between two media.

00:01:50.256 --> 00:01:53.756
While the last part of the incident front travels a distance v one times delta

00:01:53.756 --> 00:01:57.696
t, the first transmitted part travels v two times delta t.

00:01:58.716 --> 00:02:00.916
The triangles share the relevant diagonal length.

00:02:02.016 --> 00:02:07.596
Their sine ratio gives sine i over sine r equal to v one over v two.

00:02:07.596 --> 00:02:12.556
Since v equals c over n, that speed ratio is n two over n one.

00:02:12.556 --> 00:02:17.056
Rearranging gives Snell's law: n one sine i equals n two sine r.

00:02:17.936 --> 00:02:22.036
If the second speed is smaller, its front advances less during that time, and

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:23.756
the transmitted ray turns toward the normal.

00:02:24.956 --> 00:02:29.196
This derivation assumes homogeneous isotropic media and angles measured from

00:02:29.196 --> 00:02:30.036
the boundary normal.

00:02:31.296 --> 00:02:35.376
Frequency matches at the stationary boundary; the changed speed therefore also

00:02:35.376 --> 00:02:36.136
changes wavelength.

00:02:37.116 --> 00:02:40.816
Wavefront geometry and the earlier ray calculations are two compatible

00:02:40.816 --> 00:02:42.856
descriptions, not rival rules.

00:02:43.272 --> 00:02:46.712
When two light waves reach a point, their electric fields add.

00:02:47.192 --> 00:02:49.912
If their phases line up, the combined field is stronger.

00:02:50.792 --> 00:02:53.832
If they oppose, it can be weaker or even vanish.

00:02:54.672 --> 00:02:59.832
A steady pattern needs coherent sources: a stable relative phase and the same

00:02:59.832 --> 00:03:01.732
frequency in the elementary model.

00:03:02.872 --> 00:03:07.492
Two ordinary independent lamps do not usually maintain that relationship, so

00:03:07.492 --> 00:03:09.892
the fast changing pattern averages away.

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:14.112
Young's arrangement illuminates two narrow slits from a common source.

00:03:14.912 --> 00:03:17.312
The slits act as related secondary sources.

00:03:18.212 --> 00:03:22.712
For equal initial phase, an extra path length of one wavelength produces an

00:03:22.712 --> 00:03:25.332
extra phase of two pi and restores alignment.

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:29.932
A half wavelength path difference produces a phase difference of pi and

00:03:29.932 --> 00:03:30.372
opposition.

00:03:31.672 --> 00:03:34.612
Constructive interference occurs at path difference m times wavelength.

00:03:35.812 --> 00:03:39.512
Destructive interference occurs at m plus one half, times wavelength, for

00:03:39.512 --> 00:03:42.512
nonnegative order labels or corresponding signed positions.

00:03:43.392 --> 00:03:47.152
If the slits start with a nonzero fixed phase offset, the pattern shifts.

00:03:48.112 --> 00:03:52.932
For fully interfering, same polarization waves, intensity is I one plus I two

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:56.472
plus twice the square root of their product times cosine of the phase

00:03:56.472 --> 00:03:56.872
difference.

00:03:57.892 --> 00:04:02.232
Equal intensities of nine units each give maximum thirty six and minimum zero.

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:06.752
Different intensities of four and nine give maximum twenty five and minimum

00:04:06.752 --> 00:04:07.192
one.

00:04:08.152 --> 00:04:11.652
Unequal amplitudes therefore prevent completely dark minima, even when

00:04:11.652 --> 00:04:12.432
coherence is good.

00:04:12.576 --> 00:04:16.716
Put the slit separation at d and the screen distance at D.

00:04:17.316 --> 00:04:21.156
For a point a small vertical distance y from the central line, the path

00:04:21.156 --> 00:04:25.748
difference is approximately d times y divided by D, when the screen is far

00:04:25.748 --> 00:04:25.996
away

00:04:25.996 --> 00:04:27.256
and the angle is small.

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:30.436
Set this equal to m wavelengths for a bright fringe.

00:04:31.116 --> 00:04:33.856
Its position is m times wavelength D over d.

00:04:34.496 --> 00:04:38.876
Consecutive bright fringes are separated by beta, the fringe width, equal to

00:04:38.876 --> 00:04:40.016
wavelength D over d.

00:04:40.436 --> 00:04:44.396
The dark fringes lie halfway between those bright positions for equal initial

00:04:44.396 --> 00:04:44.816
phase.

00:04:45.516 --> 00:04:48.956
Let's calculate with wavelength five hundred nanometers, screen distance two

00:04:48.956 --> 00:04:51.096
meters, and slit separation one millimeter.

00:04:52.016 --> 00:04:55.596
Convert carefully: wavelength is five times ten to the negative seven meters,

00:04:55.596 --> 00:04:58.936
and slit separation is ten to the negative three meters.

00:04:59.956 --> 00:05:02.996
Fringe width is ten to the negative three meters, or one millimeter.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:06.456
The second bright fringe from the center is at two millimeters.

00:05:07.296 --> 00:05:11.776
Increase screen distance and the fringes spread; increase slit separation and

00:05:11.776 --> 00:05:12.556
they crowd together.

00:05:13.216 --> 00:05:17.856
If the entire propagation region is filled by a uniform medium of index one

00:05:17.856 --> 00:05:21.903
point five, frequency stays fixed and wavelength becomes smaller by that

00:05:21.903 --> 00:05:22.296
factor.

00:05:23.176 --> 00:05:26.156
Fringe width becomes about zero point six six seven millimeters.

00:05:26.856 --> 00:05:31.116
These formulas assume coherent equal phase illumination and the distant screen

00:05:31.116 --> 00:05:34.616
small angle geometry, not every possible two source arrangement.

00:05:34.944 --> 00:05:39.064
Now cover one slit with a thin transparent parallel sheet.

00:05:39.964 --> 00:05:42.844
Light travels through material instead of the same thickness of air.

00:05:43.424 --> 00:05:48.384
The extra optical path is n minus one, times sheet thickness t, taking air

00:05:48.384 --> 00:05:49.144
index as one.

00:05:49.704 --> 00:05:53.784
It is optical path, not simply the physical distance between slit and screen.

00:05:54.584 --> 00:05:58.375
Suppose the sheet index is one point five and its thickness is two

00:05:58.375 --> 00:05:59.064
micrometers.

00:05:59.764 --> 00:06:01.964
The added optical path is one micrometer.

00:06:02.804 --> 00:06:07.124
With wavelength half a micrometer, that equals two wavelengths, so the pattern

00:06:07.124 --> 00:06:08.644
shifts by two fringe widths.

00:06:09.224 --> 00:06:13.224
In our one millimeter fringe example, the shift magnitude is two millimeters.

00:06:13.944 --> 00:06:15.804
Which direction does the central fringe move?

00:06:16.704 --> 00:06:19.244
Pause and picture the covered slit above the uncovered one.

00:06:19.864 --> 00:06:21.964
The sheet has added delay to the upper path.

00:06:22.484 --> 00:06:26.524
To restore equal total optical paths, the observation point moves upward,

00:06:27.304 --> 00:06:28.804
making the geometric upper path shorter.

00:06:29.484 --> 00:06:31.284
The center moves toward the covered slit.

00:06:32.064 --> 00:06:36.564
The fringe spacing itself is unchanged in this ideal thin sheet setup because

00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:39.644
the sheet contributes a nearly constant additional path difference.

00:06:40.464 --> 00:06:44.264
A sheet across the whole apparatus, or a strongly angle dependent optical

00:06:44.264 --> 00:06:44.524
path,

00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:45.884
is a different problem.

00:06:46.704 --> 00:06:51.344
Finally, don't call a missing dark fringe proof of lost coherence without

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:53.044
checking intensities and polarizations.

00:06:53.404 --> 00:06:58.624
Our checklist is source phase, optical path, wavelength, geometry, and then

00:06:58.624 --> 00:06:59.004
intensity.

00:06:59.744 --> 00:07:01.644
Those same habits will carry us into diffraction.
